# pluck — token-efficient code reading

Prefer `pluck.*` tools over Bash `cat` / `grep` / `rg` for any file inside
the indexed repo. Every pluck tool has a `--raw` mode if you need byte-exact
output — capability is never lost, only tokens.

## Tools

- `pluck.read(path)` — file content with smart outline (10x fewer tokens than cat)
- `pluck.grep(pattern)` — keyword search (wraps ripgrep; all flags work)
- `pluck.search(query)` — semantic + keyword hybrid (use when you don't know the literal)
- `pluck.symbol(name)` — read just that function/class (not the whole file)
- `pluck.peek(name)` — signature + direct callees only
- `pluck.expand(name, hop)` — symbol + callees up to N hops

## When to fall back to Bash

- File is binary (use `file <path>` first if unsure).
- You need byte-exact stdout for piping into another shell tool.
- The target is outside the indexed repo.

## Token math

Examples for an 800-line TypeScript file containing a 40-line function:

| Action | Bash | pluck |
|--------|------|-------|
| Understand the file | `cat`  ≈ 6,000 tok | `pluck.read` ≈ 600 tok |
| Read one function | `cat` + scroll ≈ 6,000 tok | `pluck.symbol` ≈ 300 tok |
| Just the signature | `cat` + scroll ≈ 6,000 tok | `pluck.peek` ≈ 15 tok |

Use pluck by default. Use Bash for the cases above.
